Z||000000||CozmoPooky||02-09-2001||08:35 AM||||Not a cozmetic surgery problem (bad pun) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>My '87 Duke Coupe has an '86 aero nose installed on it and it is starting to sag in on both sides of the Fiero badge.  A search revealed nothing, has anyone experienced this problem and if so come up with any solutions?<BR>(The rest of the nose looks fine...)<P>------------------<BR>CozmoPooky<BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>'87 Duke Coupe     <BR>'85 2M6 Targa<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bothfierossig1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||02-09-2001||10:48 AM||||Sorry, don't have the answer but my 85GT does the same thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||02-09-2001||11:32 AM||||Wasn't there an egg crate crush panel in back of the nose?  I am not sure of how much support that would give to the nose shape and resulting sag.  But some sort of brace could be missing, broken or added to get rid of sag.||||reg||3
Z||000003||rogergarrison||02-09-2001||12:00 PM||||If theres room under the sagging panel, u can take the nose off and could use some flexible bumper repair epoxy and lay the nose cn its top with a piece of metal over the area and lay a weight like cinder block on it over nite. Ive done a few sagging Corvette noses that way. I dont know how much area is supported by the honeycomb on Fiero aero nose. makes sure you sand and clean the area first or the epoxy wont stick. they have a special spray after its sanded called adhesion promoter, to use to form a better bond on the spot before you glue on the panel.||||reg||1
Z||000004||CozmoPooky||02-09-2001||05:17 PM||||Thanx Lar, I'll dismantle it and check if the PO "left off" any stuff or didn't put it together right when he installed the fascia.  The "sag" is very shallow ('bout 1/4") you can really notice it right after a fresh coat of wax. If surgery is required, I'll do something along the lines Roger suggested.<BR>(Rhinoplasty?) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>  ||||reg||14
Z||000005||Neverfinished||02-09-2001||05:39 PM||||I had the same problem on my 84. I went and got a nose of an 87 i believe and it was made out of a thicker plastic or a different plastic altogether. The thicker one was yellow plastic and the 84 was black or vise versa. You might just have to get a new nose.<BR>but the cinder block would be the best way if you're not replacing it in my opinion||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||02-09-2001||06:34 PM||||I was talking with Ed Parks in Louisville and he told me about a body shop nearby that "heat treats" various body panels for him. Supposedly this process returns the panels  to their original shape. Ed seem quite happy with the results.||||reg||1
